// Concept texts, batch 7 (names JEALOUSY to NEGGING).
// Row: [name, group code, short, feels, looks, misreading, help]. An empty help text uses the group's default.
// Group codes: P phobia, A anxiety, M mood, D disorder, T thinking pattern, C control or manipulation tactic,
// R trait or relationship pattern, V harmful habit, U strength or virtue, K practice or tool,
// F fascination or urge, B body or medical.
export default [
  ["JEALOUSY", "M", "A painful feeling of threat when you fear losing someone, or when someone else seems to have what you want.", "Jealousy can bring a hot, tight feeling in the chest and a need to check, compare or react. It often comes from fear of loss or low self-worth.", "Checking a partner's phone, asking repeated questions about who they saw, or sulking when a friend gets attention.", "Feeling jealous is not proof that someone is cheating or that you are to blame. It is harmful when it leads to control, spying or put-downs.", ""],
  ["JEALOUSY INDUCTION", "C", "Deliberately making someone jealous, through flirting, hints or invented rivals, to keep them unsure and off balance.", "The target may feel anxious, hurt and constantly on edge. Trust can erode as they wonder what is real and what is being staged.", "Mentioning an ex or a new admirer with no clear reason, flirting in front of a partner, or saying 'I could have anyone' to create doubt.", "Feeling jealous at times is human, and it does not mean the other person is guilty. Creating jealousy on purpose is manipulation, not a sign of love.", "If this is happening to you, a counselor or domestic violence advocate can help you plan. The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["JERUSALEM SYNDROME", "D", "A rare condition where a person develops intense religious beliefs or delusions while in Jerusalem.", "The person may feel chosen, urgent or certain of a religious mission. Others around them may feel worried, confused or afraid for them.", "Sudden preaching, dressing in religious costume, or refusing food or sleep to carry out a belief. Family and friends often notice a sharp change.", "Strong faith or a deeply moving visit is not the same as this condition. It needs medical care when beliefs cause distress, danger or loss of daily function.", ""],
  ["JOURNAL", "K", "A notebook or document where you write thoughts, feelings and events to understand them better.", "Writing can feel awkward at first, then steady and clearing. Many people feel lighter once their thoughts are on the page.", "A person sitting quietly with a notebook, writing a few lines by hand or typing at the end of the day.", "Journaling does not need good writing or daily perfection. It is not a place to replay harm over and over, and a journal cannot replace professional help.", ""],
  ["JOURNALING", "K", "The regular practice of writing down your thoughts, feelings and daily events, often for reflection or stress relief.", "Putting feelings into words can bring a sense of order and relief. Some sessions feel calm, while others bring up hard memories.", "Someone writing for ten or fifteen minutes at a set time, sometimes with a list of prompts, then closing the book and moving on.", "Writing about hard feelings helps many people, but it is not the same as getting support. It is harmful when it becomes endless rumination that deepens distress.", ""],
  ["JOY RECONNECTION", "K", "A practice of gently returning to activities, people and sights that once brought you pleasure or meaning.", "Trying things again can feel forced or flat at first. A small spark of pleasure may appear, then grow slowly over days.", "Someone choosing a favorite song, a walk, or an old hobby on purpose, and noticing how it lands rather than expecting a big change.", "Joy does not come back on command, and feeling flat is not a personal failure. If low mood lasts for weeks, that deserves support from a clinician.", ""],
  ["JOYFUL", "U", "Feeling bright, glad and open, with a sense that life is good in this moment.", "Joy can feel light and warm in the body, with energy that spills into laughter or kindness. It often fades, and that is normal.", "Smiling easily, singing or laughing without effort, and greeting others with warmth in an ordinary day.", "Joy is not the same as being happy all the time or pretending that pain is gone. It is a feeling that comes and goes, and it can sit beside sadness.", ""],
  ["JOYFUL EMBODIMENT", "U", "Feeling at home in your body, and letting joy show up through breath, movement and sensation.", "Being in the body can feel warm, grounded and alive. Some people feel shy at first, and body awareness can grow slowly with practice.", "Someone moving freely, stretching in the morning, or dancing around the kitchen, and seeming relaxed in their own skin.", "Feeling joyful in the body does not mean ignoring pain, illness or the need for rest. If a body feels unsafe or stuck, a doctor or therapist can help.", ""],
  ["JOYFUL GRATITUDE", "U", "Feeling thankful for good things, big or small, and letting that thankfulness make life feel fuller.", "Gratitude can feel warm and steady, like a quiet sense of being looked after. It can also feel uncomfortable when life is hard.", "Thanking someone sincerely, naming a good moment at dinner, or writing down a few things that went well at the end of the day.", "Gratitude does not mean that every hard feeling must be hidden or that you should accept harm. You can be thankful and still want change.", ""],
  ["JOYFUL LIFE ACCEPTANCE", "U", "Accepting life as it is, including its uncertainty and loss, and finding joy within it.", "Acceptance can bring a sense of relief, as if you have stopped fighting something you cannot change. Some days it is easier than others.", "Someone saying 'this is hard, and I can still enjoy the tea,' or letting go of a plan and finding something good in the new one.", "Accepting a situation is not approval of harm or giving up on needed change. It means facing what is real so that you can choose your next step.", ""],
  ["JOYFUL MINDFUL PRESENCE", "U", "Being fully here in the moment, noticing what is around you with openness and a light heart.", "Presence can feel calm and clear, with less of a pull toward the past or future. Attention may drift many times, and returning is part of it.", "Someone pausing to look at the sky, listening closely during a conversation, or savoring food without rushing to the next task.", "Being present does not mean ignoring problems or never planning ahead. It is a way of meeting the moment, which can help you plan with a clearer mind.", ""],
  ["JOYFUL SELF-EXPRESSION", "U", "Sharing who you are through creativity, voice or style, in a way that feels honest and enjoyable.", "Expressing yourself can feel freeing and alive, though it may also bring a fear of being judged. Each small step can build confidence over time.", "Someone drawing, writing a song, wearing a favorite color, or speaking up in a group with a clear and warm voice.", "Self-expression is not the same as oversharing or saying whatever comes to mind. It works best when it respects both your truth and other people.", ""],
  ["JOYFUL SURRENDER", "U", "Letting go of control over what you cannot change, and allowing life to unfold with some ease.", "Surrender can feel like loosening a tight grip, with relief and a bit of vulnerability. It may come in waves, not as a single decision.", "Someone saying 'I have done what I can,' stepping back from a worry, or accepting a plan that did not go as hoped without a fight.", "Surrender is not the same as giving up or ignoring problems. It is harmful when it means staying in an abusive or unsafe situation.", ""],
  ["JUBILANT", "M", "A bright, triumphant happiness, often after success, relief or good news that feels worth celebrating.", "Jubilation can feel buoyant, loud and full of energy. It may leave you tired afterward, and that is a normal part of big emotions.", "Cheering, dancing, calling loved ones with good news, or talking fast and smiling widely after a win or a relief.", "Feeling jubilant does not mean that everything is solved or that other people must feel the same. Sharing the moment is kind, and so is making room for others' feelings.", ""],
  ["KIND", "U", "Caring about others and showing it through gentle words and actions, even when it is small.", "Kindness can feel warm and steady, both for the one who gives it and the one who receives it. It can also feel tender when you are tired.", "Holding a door, checking on a neighbor, or speaking gently when someone makes a mistake, without making a show of it.", "Kindness is not the same as saying yes to everything or letting others cross your limits. Being kind to yourself and others includes honest boundaries.", ""],
  ["KINDNESS", "U", "A caring way of treating people and yourself, shown through patience, generosity and respect.", "Kindness can bring a sense of connection and a calmer heart. Being kind to yourself can feel awkward at first, then more natural with practice.", "Listening without interrupting, saying thank you, and speaking to yourself with the same care you would give a friend.", "Kindness is not weakness, and it is not the same as hiding anger or hurt. Honest words can be kind, and so can a firm limit.", ""],
  ["KLEINE-LEVIN SYNDROME", "D", "A rare condition with episodes of very long sleep, confusion and altered behavior, often in teenagers or young adults.", "During an episode, the person may feel foggy, drowsy and disconnected. Afterward, they may feel confused about what happened and worried for others.", "Sleeping for most of the day for days or weeks, eating much more than usual, and showing unusual irritability or confusion, then returning to normal.", "Needing a lot of sleep is common and is not the same as this condition. It is rare, and it needs a doctor to rule out other causes and plan care.", ""],
  ["KLEPTOMANIA", "D", "A rare impulse-control disorder marked by repeated urges to steal items that are not needed or wanted.", "The urge can build as tension, then ease briefly after taking something. Guilt, shame and worry often follow, and the cycle can repeat.", "Taking small items from stores or friends, often hiding them or returning them later, and feeling distressed about the act.", "Shoplifting is not always kleptomania, and it is not a sign of bad character. The pattern needs care when it repeats, causes distress, or puts you at legal risk.", ""],
  ["KLINEFELTER SYNDROME", "B", "A genetic condition where a person is born with an extra X chromosome, which can affect growth and health.", "Some people feel tired, shy or unsure about their body or energy. Learning and emotional health can also be affected, and this can be hard to name.", "Often taller limbs, lower energy, delayed puberty or lower muscle strength, though many people show few signs at all.", "Having this condition does not limit who a person can be. Many people live full lives with regular checkups and the right support.", "A doctor who knows genetic conditions should see you, especially for growth, puberty or fertility concerns. If it is affecting your mood or safety, call or text 988 in the US. In an emergency, call 911."],
  ["KLISMAPHILIA", "F", "A sexual interest in enemas or the sensations and rituals linked to them.", "The interest can feel private, intense or confusing, especially if others do not share it. Some people feel shame, while others feel at ease with it.", "Seeking out related content or talking about it often, or feeling preoccupied with the topic when sexual feelings arise.", "Having a sexual interest is not the same as acting on it. Acting without consent is harm, and it is a problem when the interest causes distress.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis."],
  ["KLÜVER-BUCY SYNDROME", "D", "A rare brain condition, often from injury or disease, that changes behavior, memory and how the senses are read.", "The person may feel restless, unsure of what things mean, or driven to touch and explore things. Family may feel worried and unsure how to help.", "Strong urges to put objects in the mouth, unusual calm or rage, changes in appetite and sexual interest, and trouble naming familiar things.", "Changes in behavior after a brain injury are not a choice or a character flaw. This condition is rare and needs a doctor to find the cause and plan care.", ""],
  ["KNEE-JERK REFLEX", "B", "A quick, automatic leg kick when the tendon below the knee is tapped, used as a simple nerve check.", "The reflex is quick and automatic, so most people do not feel it as a decision. It can feel like a small jolt when a doctor tests it.", "A doctor taps the tendon just below the kneecap with a small rubber hammer, and the lower leg kicks forward briefly.", "A normal reflex is not a sign of brain trouble, and a reflex that is weak or very strong does not always mean a serious illness. A doctor needs to look at the full picture.", ""],
  ["KORO SYNDROME", "D", "An anxiety condition, seen in some cultures, in which a person fears their genitals will shrink into the body.", "The fear can feel sudden and overwhelming, with panic and a sense of losing control. Others may gather around and add to the alarm.", "Repeated checking, holding or pulling at the genitals, and intense distress, sometimes in group outbreaks where fear spreads.", "Worry about the body is common, but this condition is an anxiety pattern, not proof of physical damage. It needs a doctor and mental health care.", ""],
  ["LACTOPHILIA", "F", "A sexual interest in breast milk or lactation, which can vary widely from person to person.", "The interest can feel private, sometimes confusing or embarrassing, especially when others do not share it. Shame often grows in secrecy.", "Strong focus on breastfeeding or lactation in a sexual way, seeking related content often, or pressing others to share it.", "Having a sexual interest is not the same as acting on it. Acting without consent is harm, and any attempt to pressure another person is never acceptable.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis."],
  ["LATAH SYNDROME", "D", "A culture-linked startle condition where a person copies sounds, words or actions after being startled.", "A startle can bring a sudden jolt and confusion. The person may feel they cannot stop the echoing, and embarrassment can follow.", "Repeating words or movements of others after a surprise, sometimes following a command without meaning to, and then becoming embarrassed.", "Being startled and repeating a phrase can happen to anyone in a moment. The condition is rare, and it is worth a doctor's visit if it is frequent or disruptive.", ""],
  ["LEARNED HELPLESSNESS", "T", "A belief that you cannot change outcomes, learned after repeated failure or loss of control.", "Efforts can feel pointless, and trying again may seem futile. Hope can fade, and small tasks may feel much heavier than they are.", "Giving up early, saying 'it does not matter what I do', or not trying even when a change is possible and safe.", "Feeling helpless is not the same as being unable to act. Many situations do change, and it helps to notice which parts you can still influence.", ""],
  ["LESCH-NYHAN SYNDROME", "B", "A rare inherited condition that affects movement, speech and behavior, often starting in infancy.", "The person may feel stiff, uncomfortable or unable to control their movements. Family members often feel worried and overwhelmed.", "Involuntary movements, delayed development, and self-injury such as biting the lips or fingers, often seen in early childhood.", "Self-injury in this condition is a symptom of the illness, not a choice or a sign of bad behavior. It is rare and needs specialist care from a doctor.", "This rare condition needs a specialist doctor, so do not wait to seek care. If it is affecting your mood or safety, call or text 988 in the US. In an emergency, call 911."],
  ["LEWY BODY DEMENTIA", "D", "A type of dementia caused by abnormal protein deposits in the brain, affecting thinking, movement and sleep.", "The person may feel confused, anxious or unsure what they see, and sleep may feel restless or disturbed. Family may feel grief as changes build.", "Hallucinations, daytime drowsiness, slowed movement, shaking, and acting out dreams, along with memory and attention changes.", "Memory slips are common with age and are not always dementia. This condition is progressive and needs a doctor's assessment, and early care can help.", ""],
  ["LIBERATED", "U", "Feeling free from a limit, habit or pressure that once held you back, and choosing your own path.", "Freedom can feel light and energizing, with a new sense of choice. It may also bring fear, since new choices come with responsibility.", "Someone saying no to an unwanted commitment, leaving a harmful habit, or setting their own schedule with calm confidence.", "Feeling liberated is not the same as ignoring others or doing whatever you like. Real freedom includes care for yourself and the people your choices affect.", ""],
  ["LIFE FORCE REAWAKENING", "U", "A renewed sense of energy, interest and aliveness after a time of dullness, grief or exhaustion.", "Energy can return slowly, sometimes as a small spark of interest. It may feel fragile at first, and setbacks are part of the process.", "Someone starting to go outside again, returning to a hobby, or joining in conversation after a long quiet stretch.", "Renewed energy is not a sign that all pain is over, and it does not need to arrive all at once. If low mood or exhaustion lasts, a doctor or therapist can help.", ""],
  ["LIGHTHEARTED", "M", "A relaxed, cheerful state of mind where worries feel small and humor comes easily.", "The mood can feel easy and open, with more patience and fun. It may come and go with sleep, stress and the day's events.", "Laughing easily, teasing kindly, making plans with enthusiasm, or handling small setbacks with a shrug and a smile.", "Being lighthearted is not the same as ignoring serious problems or never feeling sad. It is a healthy mood, and it can sit beside real concerns.", ""],
  ["SEPARATION ANXIETY DISORDER", "D", "An intense, lasting fear of being apart from people one is attached to, beyond what is typical for the age.", "Being apart can bring panic, dread and a strong urge to check in. The fear may feel too big for the situation, and it can be exhausting.", "Clinging, frequent calls or messages, refusing to leave home or go to school, and distress at bedtime or when a loved one leaves.", "Some worry at separation is normal, especially for young children. The disorder is about fear that is persistent, intense and affects daily life, and treatment can help.", ""],
  ["LIMERENCE", "F", "An intense, obsessive state of romantic longing, with constant thoughts and hope for a return of feelings.", "It can feel thrilling and painful at once, with waves of hope and anxiety. Sleep, focus and self-worth can suffer when feelings go unreturned.", "Replaying small moments, checking social media for signs, reading meaning into tiny gestures, and feeling crushed when there is no reply.", "Strong romantic feelings are normal and not a flaw. Having the feeling is not the same as acting on it, and pursuing someone who has said no is harm.", ""],
  ["LIMITLESS EXPANSION", "U", "A sense of growing without fixed limits, opening to new ideas, skills and possibilities.", "Growth can feel exciting and open, with room to try new things. It can also feel overwhelming when too many goals compete for time.", "Someone signing up for a class, trying a new skill, or saying yes to a new challenge while still making room for rest.", "Growth does not mean having no limits, and it does not require doing everything at once. Sustainable growth respects the body, time and real needs.", ""],
  ["LIMITLESS SELF-BELIEF", "U", "A strong trust in your own ability to reach goals, even when others doubt you or the path is hard.", "This belief can feel energizing and steady, with a willingness to try again. It may fade after failure, and that is part of learning.", "Taking on hard challenges, speaking up with confidence, and bouncing back from setbacks with a clear next step.", "Self-belief is healthy, but it is not the same as ignoring feedback or evidence. Belief becomes harmful when it stops you from learning or asking for help.", ""],
  ["LISTENER", "U", "Someone who pays close attention to others, hearing their words and feelings without rushing to judge.", "Being listened to can feel calm and safe, as if you matter. Listening well can also feel tiring, and it is fine to take breaks.", "Nodding, asking gentle questions, reflecting back what was said, and waiting before answering instead of planning a reply.", "Listening is not the same as agreeing or fixing every problem. It is harmful when one person always listens and never has their own needs heard.", ""],
  ["LOCKED-IN SYNDROME", "B", "A rare condition where a person is fully aware but cannot move or speak, except perhaps for their eyes.", "The person may be fully awake and aware while unable to move. Fear, frustration and isolation can be intense, and communication takes great effort.", "Complete paralysis of the body except the eyes, often after a stroke or injury to the brainstem, with eye movement used to communicate.", "Being unable to move does not mean a person has lost thought or feeling. It is a rare, serious condition that needs urgent medical care and ongoing support.", "Locked-in syndrome is rare and needs urgent medical care, so call emergency services right away. If it is affecting your mood or safety, call or text 988 in the US. In an emergency, call 911."],
  ["LOGOMANIAC", "F", "A strong pull toward words, speech and wordplay, sometimes to the point of being hard to stop.", "Words can feel exciting, playful and deeply satisfying. Sometimes the urge to speak or write can feel hard to pause, and others may tire of it.", "Talking at length, collecting unusual words, repeating phrases, or turning every conversation into a language game.", "Loving language is a healthy interest, not a sign of illness. It becomes a concern when speech or writing drives out sleep, work or relationships.", ""],
  ["LONELINESS", "M", "A painful feeling that connection is missing, even when you are around other people.", "Loneliness can feel empty, heavy and sometimes shameful. It can make the day feel longer and make reaching out feel harder.", "Spending long stretches alone, avoiding invitations, sleeping more or less, or feeling flat when others are around.", "Being alone is not the same as being lonely, and loneliness is not a personal failure. It is common, and it is harmful when it lasts for weeks.", ""],
  ["THE LOOSE", "R", "A person who often disregards commitments, social limits or other people's trust, and seems unbothered by it.", "Being around them can feel unpredictable and tiring. You may feel you must keep checking whether plans will hold, and it can feel lonely to carry the load.", "Missed plans with no real apology, promises that quietly disappear, and careless remarks that cross lines, followed by shrugs or blame.", "Being spontaneous or relaxed is not the same as being careless with others. The pattern is harmful when it repeatedly breaks trust or puts others at risk.", ""],
  ["LOSS OF APPETITE", "B", "A reduced desire to eat, which can be caused by stress, illness, medicines or low mood.", "Food may seem unappealing, and meals can feel like a chore. Hunger may be quiet or absent, and energy can dip over the day.", "Eating much less than usual, skipping meals, losing weight, or feeling full quickly, sometimes with tiredness.", "Eating less for a day or two is common and often passes. It is not the same as a long-term problem, which can affect health and needs a doctor's care.", "Loss of appetite that lasts more than a couple of weeks, or leads to weight loss, needs a doctor's visit. If it is affecting your mood or safety, call or text 988 in the US. In an emergency, call 911."],
  ["LOSS OF INTEREST (ANHEDONIA)", "M", "A reduced ability to feel pleasure or interest in things that once felt enjoyable.", "Favorite things can feel flat, distant or not worth the effort. The gap between what you used to enjoy and what you feel now can feel lonely.", "Dropping hobbies, turning down time with friends, or going through the motions of daily life without much joy in them.", "Feeling less interested for a while is common during stress or grief. It is a concern when it lasts for weeks, and it is often a sign of depression that treatment can help.", "Loss of interest that lasts for weeks needs attention from a doctor or therapist. If you have thoughts of ending your life, call or text 988 in the US now, and call 911 if you are in danger."],
  ["LOVE BOMBER", "R", "A person who showers someone with fast affection and gifts to build quick trust, then uses that bond to control.", "Early attention can feel exciting and flattering, then confusing when the warmth turns to demands. Being around them can feel like walking on eggshells.", "Declaring love or plans very early, pushing for constant contact, gifts that create a sense of debt, then sudden coldness or criticism.", "Warmth and romance are not the same as love bombing. The pattern is harmful when intense affection is used to create dependence and then to control.", "If this is happening to you, a counselor or domestic violence advocate can help you plan. The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["LOVE BOMBING", "C", "Overwhelming someone with affection, attention and gifts early on, to create quick dependence and control.", "The target may feel swept up, flattered and special at first. Later, they may feel guilty or confused when the closeness comes with conditions.", "Very fast talk of commitment, constant messages, grand gestures, and then pulling back or criticizing when expectations are not met.", "Kindness and romance are not manipulation by themselves. It is harmful when attention is used to build dependence and then to control choices.", "If this is happening to you, a counselor or domestic violence advocate can help you plan. The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["LOVEBOMBOPHILE", "F", "A pull toward the rush of being showered with fast, intense affection, even when it moves faster than trust.", "Being showered with attention can feel like being chosen and special. The rush can make slower, steadier love seem dull by comparison.", "Seeking out partners who move very fast, or chasing the first weeks of a relationship when the excitement fades.", "Enjoying warm, eager attention is not a flaw. It becomes a concern when the rush keeps pulling you toward people who push too quickly.", ""],
  ["LOVING", "U", "Showing warmth, care and kindness toward people, including yourself, in steady and practical ways.", "Loving feels warm and open. It can bring a sense of connection, and it often makes hard days easier to carry with others.", "Checking in on a friend, listening without rushing to fix, and saying kind things even when you are tired or annoyed.", "Love is not the same as always agreeing or giving in. It is harmful when it is used to excuse mistreatment or to avoid honest limits.", ""],
  ["LOVING BOUNDARIES", "K", "A practice of setting limits with care, so you protect your time and energy without shutting people out.", "Saying no kindly can bring a flutter of guilt or worry about how the other person will react. Over time, the calm of being clear often grows.", "A short, friendly sentence such as 'I cannot take that on this week', followed by a plan or a warm closing rather than a long excuse.", "Boundaries are not punishment or walls against everyone. They become harmful only when used to cut people off unfairly or to control them.", ""],
  ["LOVING DISCIPLINE", "U", "Steady, kind structure that helps people grow, keeping rules clear while staying warm and respectful.", "Firm but caring limits can feel steadying. When warmth is clear too, a person can feel safe enough to try new things and accept correction.", "Clear expectations stated calmly, followed through consistently, with explanations and affection rather than shouting or shame.", "Discipline is not the same as punishment or control. It stops being loving when it relies on fear, humiliation or harsh force to get compliance.", ""],
  ["LOVING SELF-COMMITMENT", "U", "Keeping promises to yourself with warmth, so your choices match what matters to you over time.", "Keeping a promise to yourself can feel grounding and quietly proud. Breaking one can bring disappointment, and it helps to meet that with patience.", "Following through on a small habit most days, adjusting a plan without giving up, and speaking to yourself kindly after a slip.", "Self-commitment is not harsh self-control or never missing a day. It becomes harmful when it turns into shame, perfectionism or punishment for one mistake.", ""],
  ["LOVING SELF-DISCIPLINE", "U", "Gentle self-control that helps you choose what supports your health, goals and relationships.", "Self-discipline can feel calm and purposeful when it is gentle. When it turns harsh, it can feel like an inner critic pushing you to keep going no matter what.", "Planning ahead, pausing before an impulse, and returning to a healthy routine after a rough day without a long lecture.", "Self-discipline is not self-punishment or doing everything at once. It becomes harmful when it ignores rest, or when every slip is met with cruelty.", ""],
  ["LOVING-KINDNESS MEDITATION (METTA)", "K", "A meditation practice that repeats kind wishes for yourself, loved ones, neutral people and difficult people.", "Repeating kind phrases can feel soft and a little awkward at first. Many people notice warmth grow, and some feel resistance toward certain people, which is normal.", "Sitting quietly and silently repeating phrases such as 'may you be well' and 'may you be at ease', then widening them to others.", "This practice does not require you to feel love on demand or to forgive harm right away. It is gentle training, not a duty to pretend.", ""],
  ["LOYAL", "U", "Staying faithful and supportive to people, causes or promises, especially when things are difficult.", "Loyalty can feel warm and secure, like knowing someone has your back. It can also feel heavy when it is asked for without fairness or respect.", "Showing up for a friend during a hard week, keeping a confidence, and speaking up for someone who is not in the room.", "Loyalty is not the same as staying silent about harm or following someone into mistreatment. It is harmful when it asks you to give up your safety or honesty.", ""],
  ["LUDOMANIAC", "V", "A strong urge or pull toward gambling, where betting takes up more thought and money than it should.", "The urge to gamble can feel exciting and hopeful, with a strong pull to chase the next win. Losses often bring stress, shame and a need to try again.", "Thinking about bets often, borrowing money to play, hiding losses, or returning to gambling after promising to stop.", "Enjoying a game or a small bet is not the same as gambling harm. It becomes a concern when betting causes debt, secrecy, or trouble with work and relationships.", ""],
  ["LUST", "V", "A strong desire for sex or sexual pleasure that can cause harm when it drives choices against your values or consent.", "Strong desire can feel urgent and hard to resist, and it can crowd out other thoughts. Afterward, some people feel regret, emptiness or shame.", "Constant sexual thoughts that disrupt work or sleep, repeated risky choices, or using people or porn to escape other feelings.", "Sexual desire is a normal part of being human, and it is not shameful in itself. It becomes harmful when it pressures others or ignores consent and wellbeing.", ""],
  ["LUSTOPHILE", "F", "A pull toward intense sexual desire, where seeking out sexual arousal often takes a central place in life.", "Strong desire can feel exciting and alive, and it may be hard to ignore. Some people feel pulled back again and again, even when it leaves them drained.", "Frequent sexual thoughts that crowd out other plans, seeking new partners or content often, or feeling restless when desire is not met.", "Having a strong sexual interest is not the same as acting on it. Acting without consent is harm, and any sexual choice should respect the other person fully.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis."],
  ["MACABROPHILE", "F", "A pull toward the macabre, such as death, decay, gore or dark history, as a source of fascination.", "Dark or morbid topics can feel gripping and strangely calming to some people. The fascination can also bring unease or a heavier mood afterward.", "Seeking out true crime, morbid art or grim details often, and joking about death in a way that makes others uneasy.", "Interest in dark topics is common and can be a healthy way to explore fear or history. It becomes a concern when it leaves you with lasting dread.", ""],
  ["MACHIAVELLIAN", "R", "A person who plans and manipulates others for gain, often with cold strategy and little concern for their feelings.", "Being around a Machiavellian person can feel like being handled rather than heard. You may notice you are being steered, and trust can slowly erode.", "Quiet information gathering, playing people against each other, flattery before a request, and hiding real aims behind a friendly face.", "Strategic thinking and careful planning are not automatically manipulative. It is harmful when it uses people or deceives them for control.", ""],
  ["MACROPHILIA", "F", "A pull toward very large bodies or objects, which for some people is a sexual interest or a recurring fantasy.", "Large size can feel impressive, exciting or dreamlike in fantasy. For some people the interest is strong and hard to set aside.", "Repeated fantasy themes about very large people or objects, or a steady pull toward content that centers on size.", "Having an interest in fantasy or size is not the same as acting on it. Acting without consent is harm, and real people deserve respect and choice.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis."],
  ["MADNESSOPHILE", "F", "A pull toward madness, such as stories, images or ideas about severe mental breakdown, as a source of fascination.", "Stories about madness can feel intriguing, scary and emotionally charged at once. Some people feel a strange closeness to characters who seem to lose their grip.", "Seeking out content about psychosis or breakdown often, or describing mental illness mainly as dramatic or thrilling.", "Curiosity about mental health is common and can build empathy. It becomes harmful when it turns real suffering into entertainment or hardens stigma.", ""],
  ["MAJOR DISORDER (DEMENTIA)", "D", "Major neurocognitive disorder, the diagnostic term for dementia, where memory and thinking decline affect daily life.", "Losing words, names or track of time can feel frightening and frustrating. People living with it and those close to them often feel grief as well as worry.", "Forgetting recent conversations, getting lost in familiar places, trouble managing money or meals, and changes in mood or judgment.", "Forgetting things now and then is common and not always dementia. Any ongoing change needs a doctor's evaluation, since some causes can be treated.", ""],
  ["MALADAPTIVE DAYDREAMING", "F", "Frequent, vivid daydreams that replace real activity, relationships or goals, often to escape stress or loneliness.", "Daydreams can feel vivid, comforting and more rewarding than real life. Coming back to the present can bring disappointment, guilt or a sense of lost time.", "Hours spent in imagined scenes, missed tasks, losing track of conversations, or preferring fantasy worlds to daily responsibilities.", "Daydreaming is a normal and often creative habit. It becomes maladaptive when it crowds out work, sleep or connection, or when it causes distress.", ""],
  ["MALIGNANT NARCISSIST", "R", "A person with narcissistic traits who also shows cruelty, deceit and enjoyment of others' distress or control.", "Being around a malignant narcissist can feel frightening, confusing and exhausting. You may feel blamed for everything and afraid of their anger or contempt.", "Cruel put-downs, punishing silence, public humiliation, and lies that are repeated until the target doubts their own memory.", "Narcissistic traits exist on a spectrum, and diagnosis is a clinical matter, not a label to throw at someone. The pattern is harmful when it causes ongoing fear or harm.", "If this is happening to you, a counselor or domestic violence advocate can help you plan. The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["MALINGERING", "C", "Faking or exaggerating illness or symptoms to gain something, such as time off, money, medicine or sympathy.", "If you are on the receiving end, you may feel doubted, pressured to cover for someone, or unsure whether to trust what you see. Honest concern can start to feel risky.", "Dramatic, shifting symptoms, pressure for medical notes or medicines, and reluctance to see a second opinion or return to work.", "Being unwell is not the same as faking it, and symptoms can be hard to explain. It is harmful when illness is invented to deceive others or take resources.", "If this is happening to you, a counselor or domestic violence advocate can help you plan. The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["MANIA", "D", "A sustained episode of very high, fast or irritable mood with racing thoughts, often part of bipolar disorder.", "Mania can feel like boundless energy, sharp ideas and no need for sleep. Many people later feel exhausted, embarrassed or frightened by what they did.", "Little sleep without tiredness, rapid speech, big plans or spending, grandiose beliefs, and irritability with people who try to slow things down.", "Being energetic or happy is not mania, and most high moods do not mean illness. Mania needs a doctor's care, especially when it disrupts sleep or safety.", "A doctor needs to evaluate this, so please book a visit soon. If it is affecting your mood or safety, call or text 988 in the US. In an emergency, call 911."],
  ["MANNERISTIC", "B", "Having stylized, repeated or exaggerated gestures, postures or speech habits that look set and unusual.", "A person with mannerisms may feel self-conscious or simply comfortable in their habits. Others may find the repetition puzzling or distracting.", "Repeated odd movements, fixed postures, exaggerated expressions, or speech that seems rehearsed and rigid.", "Mannerisms are often harmless personal habits. A new or worsening change in movement is a medical sign, not a personality quirk, and needs checking.", "A doctor needs to evaluate this, so please book a visit soon. If it is affecting your mood or safety, call or text 988 in the US. In an emergency, call 911."],
  ["MANUFACTURED", "C", "Creating a false crisis, deadline or emergency so someone feels they must act now, without time to think.", "Under manufactured urgency you may feel rushed, panicked and pressed to decide before you can check facts. Doubt and guilt may follow the choice.", "Invented deadlines, 'you have to decide tonight', dramatic claims that something is about to go wrong, and anger when you ask questions.", "Real deadlines and emergencies do happen, and asking for a quick answer is not always manipulation. It is harmful when the urgency is made up to stop you thinking.", "If this is happening to you, a counselor or domestic violence advocate can help you plan. The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["MARFAN SYNDROME", "B", "A rare inherited condition that affects connective tissue, which can involve the heart, eyes, bones and blood vessels.", "Symptoms can come and go, and there may be worry about heart or eye checks. Fatigue, joint pain or a sense of unease are common experiences.", "Very tall, long limbs, flexible joints, a curved spine, or eye and heart changes noticed during a check-up.", "Being tall or flexible does not mean someone has Marfan syndrome. It is rare, and a doctor must confirm it with specific tests and regular monitoring.", "A doctor needs to evaluate this, so please book a visit soon. If it is affecting your mood or safety, call or text 988 in the US. In an emergency, call 911."],
  ["MARIONETTEOPHILE", "F", "A pull toward puppets and marionettes, such as liking to watch, collect or handle them as a source of fascination.", "Puppets can feel uncanny and charming at the same time. The fascination may bring calm or curiosity, and sometimes a little unease.", "Collecting or watching puppet videos often, or feeling drawn to the movements of figures that seem controlled by strings.", "Liking puppets is a normal interest. It becomes a concern only when it takes over daily life or leads to harm.", ""],
  ["MARTYR", "R", "A person who sacrifices themselves openly and expects others to notice, thank them or repay them.", "Being around a martyr can feel like being guilted into agreement. You may feel obligated to make up for every sacrifice they claim.", "Constant reminders of what they gave up, sighing or silent suffering, and hints that others will regret not appreciating them.", "Caring for others and giving your time is good and is not martyrdom. It is harmful when sacrifice is used to make others feel guilty or to avoid asking for what is needed.", ""],
  ["MARTYR COMPLEX", "T", "A habit of seeing yourself as always sacrificing for others, while feeling unseen or unfairly treated.", "A martyr complex can bring a steady sense of being overlooked and resentful. Some people feel proud of their suffering, then lonely and tired.", "Thinking 'no one else would do this', keeping score of favors, and turning down help while still resenting being left alone.", "Feeling overloaded or underappreciated is real and worth addressing. It becomes harmful when it locks you into self-sacrifice and blocks honest requests for support.", ""],
  ["MARTYR PLAY", "C", "Performing suffering or sacrifice to win sympathy, favors or control, often to avoid responsibility.", "If you are on the receiving end, you may feel guilty, obligated and unsure whether a request is fair. Each refusal can bring a wave of blame.", "Dramatic sighs, 'after everything I have done for you', tallying sacrifices, and a sudden crisis whenever you set a limit.", "Real hardship deserves care, and needing help is not manipulation. It is harmful when suffering is performed to control others or to dodge accountability.", "If this is happening to you, a counselor or domestic violence advocate can help you plan. The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["MASK SLIPPING", "R", "The moment when someone's charming or careful public face cracks and their harsher side briefly shows.", "Seeing the mask slip can be unsettling, like a sudden chill. You may doubt what you saw, then feel a need to explain it away.", "A friendly person suddenly snaps at a server or partner, sneers at someone who cannot help, then smiles again as if nothing happened.", "A single bad moment does not define a person, and everyone gets irritable. It is a warning sign when harshness keeps coming back and is followed by pressure to keep it secret.", ""],
  ["MASKING", "T", "Hiding your true feelings, traits or needs to fit in, often by copying what others expect.", "Masking can feel exhausting and lonely, like wearing a costume all day. Some people feel relief when they can finally drop it with someone safe.", "Forcing a smile when upset, copying social cues, rehearsing conversations, and crashing after social events.", "Adjusting your behavior in some settings is a normal social skill. It becomes harmful when it hides pain, needs or safety for long periods.", ""],
  ["MASOCHISTIC", "R", "A person who repeatedly seeks out pain, criticism or mistreatment, and may believe they deserve it.", "A person with this pattern may feel strangely comforted by hurt, or convinced they deserve it. Being around them can leave others feeling helpless and frustrated.", "Staying in harmful relationships, apologizing for being mistreated, or choosing setbacks and criticism again and again.", "Enduring hardship is not the same as wanting harm. Repeated self-punishment is worth exploring with support, not just with willpower.", "If you have thoughts of ending your life, call or text 988 in the US now, and call 911 if you are in danger."],
  ["SEXUAL MASOCHISM", "D", "A sexual interest in receiving pain, humiliation or restraint, which can become a condition when it causes distress.", "Fantasies of pain or submission may feel exciting or private, sometimes with shame or confusion. Distress can grow when the interest feels out of control.", "Repeated focus on pain or humiliation in sexual thoughts, or seeking risky encounters that go beyond what feels safe.", "Having this interest is not the same as acting on it. Acting without clear consent is harm, and any play should be safe and agreed to by everyone involved.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis."],
  ["MEGALOMANIAC", "R", "A person with an intense, unrealistic belief in their own power, importance or brilliance, often needing control.", "Being around a megalomaniac can feel like being dismissed or pushed aside. Their plans may sweep others along, and disagreement can feel risky.", "Speaking about future triumphs as certain, expecting special treatment, dismissing expertise, and reacting badly to any challenge.", "Ambition and confidence are healthy in measured amounts. It becomes harmful when it ignores other people's needs, rights or safety.", ""],
  ["MEGALOPHOBE", "P", "A strong fear of large objects or huge spaces, which can cause panic or avoidance.", "Large objects or wide open spaces can bring a sudden tightness, dizziness or panic. The fear may feel out of proportion, even when you know it is not dangerous.", "Avoiding big buildings or open spaces, staying near edges, and checking exits or routes in advance.", "Fear of large things is a real and common experience, and it is not a weakness. It is worth treatment when it limits daily life or travel.", ""],
  ["MELANCHOLIC", "M", "A heavy, reflective sadness that can linger for a long time and dull enjoyment of everyday things.", "A melancholic mood can feel slow, heavy and inward. Things that once brought pleasure may seem distant, and it can be hard to explain why.", "Quiet withdrawal, slowed speech, sighing, and a flat interest in things that once mattered.", "Feeling melancholy is a normal part of life and can be meaningful. It is worth support when it lasts for weeks or affects sleep, appetite or safety.", ""],
  ["MELANCHOLOPHILE", "F", "A pull toward melancholy, where sad, wistful moods or art are sought out and savored.", "Melancholy can feel tender, beautiful and oddly comforting. Some people feel more connected to others through shared sadness.", "Choosing sad music, films or journal entries again and again, and staying with heavy moods instead of seeking any lift.", "Enjoying wistful art is common and can be meaningful. It becomes a concern when it deepens low mood, cuts you off from others, or brings thoughts of ending your life.", "If you have thoughts of ending your life, call or text 988 in the US now, and call 911 if you are in danger."],
  ["MELANCHOLY", "M", "A quiet, lingering sadness that is often thoughtful but can dull energy and enjoyment.", "Melancholy can feel soft, slow and heavy at once. It may come with a sense of longing or loss, and simple tasks can seem harder.", "Less talking, sighing, lost interest in favorite things, and long pauses before answering simple questions.", "Melancholy is not always depression, and sad feelings are part of being human. It is worth support when it grows heavier or starts to affect daily life.", ""],
  ["MELOMANIAC", "F", "An intense, almost compulsive love of music, where listening, collecting or seeking new songs takes over.", "Music can feel deeply moving and exciting, with a strong pull to hear it again. Some people feel restless or anxious when they cannot listen.", "Spending hours listening or buying records, rearranging plans around music, or getting irritated when it is not available.", "Loving music is healthy and common. It becomes a concern when it crowds out work, sleep or people, or causes money trouble.", ""],
  ["MENOPHILIA", "F", "A sexual interest in menstruation or menstruating people, which some people experience as a strong fascination.", "The interest may feel private, intense or confusing, sometimes with shame. Some people feel stuck between their curiosity and the wish to respect others.", "Repeated focus on menstruation in sexual thoughts, seeking content about it often, or pressuring partners to discuss or share it.", "Having an interest is not the same as acting on it. Acting without consent is harm, and no one should be pressured into sharing their body or cycle.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis."],
  ["METACOGNITION", "T", "Thinking about your own thinking, noticing your thoughts and judging how well you understand something.", "Metacognition can feel like a calm inner observer. It may also bring self-doubt when you notice patterns you wish were different.", "Pausing to ask 'what am I assuming here?', checking whether you understand a text, or noticing when you are stuck in a loop.", "Thinking about your thinking is useful, not a sign that something is wrong. It becomes unhelpful when it turns into endless self-analysis or rumination.", ""],
  ["METHEMOGLOBINEMIA", "B", "A rare blood condition in which hemoglobin cannot carry oxygen well, so lips and skin can look blue or grey.", "It can cause shortness of breath, headache, dizziness or a fast heartbeat. Some people feel unusually tired or confused, which can be frightening.", "Bluish or grey lips and skin, unexplained fatigue, and a dusky look that does not improve with rest.", "Bluish skin can have many causes, and this condition is rare. It can be serious, so it needs prompt medical care rather than guesswork.", "A doctor needs to evaluate this, so please book a visit soon. If it is affecting your mood or safety, call or text 988 in the US. In an emergency, call 911."],
  ["METHODICAL", "U", "Working in a careful, orderly way, with clear steps and attention to detail.", "A methodical approach can feel calm and steady, with a sense of control. It can also feel slow or stiff when plans must change quickly.", "Making checklists, working through tasks in order, and double-checking details before moving on.", "Method is a strength, but it is not the same as rigidity or perfectionism. It is harmful when it blocks flexibility or leads to endless checking.", ""],
  ["METROMANIAC", "F", "A compulsive urge to write poetry or verse, often producing rhymes at all hours and feeling unable to stop.", "The urge can feel exciting and flowing, with lines arriving unbidden. Some people feel anxious or drained when they cannot write.", "Writing verse constantly, neglecting other responsibilities, and feeling restless until a new poem is finished.", "Loving poetry and writing often is healthy and creative. It becomes a concern when the urge crowds out sleep, work or relationships.", ""],
  ["MILD NEUROCOGNITIVE DISORDER", "D", "A noticeable decline in memory or thinking that is more than normal aging but does not yet stop daily life.", "Losing track of words, names or appointments can feel worrying and embarrassing. Some people feel frustrated that tasks take more effort than before.", "Missing appointments more often, repeating questions, needing lists for tasks that used to be automatic, and asking for help with some steps.", "Occasional forgetfulness is common and not always a disorder. A doctor visit is worth it to check causes, since some causes can be treated.", ""],
  ["MIND READING", "T", "Assuming you know what others think or feel about you, without checking with them directly.", "Mind reading can bring anxiety, anger or hurt, because you feel sure you know someone's private view. Those guesses often feel like facts.", "Saying 'I know you think I am annoying', reacting to someone's silence as rejection, or assuming bad intent without asking.", "Noticing signals from others is a useful social skill. It is harmful when guesses are treated as facts and used to judge people unfairly.", ""],
  ["MINDFUL", "U", "Paying gentle, steady attention to what is happening now, instead of living on autopilot.", "A mindful state can feel calm and clear, as if the noise of the day has been turned down. Thoughts still come, but they seem less urgent and easier to notice.", "Someone who pauses before answering, listens fully, and notices small details like taste, weather or breath during ordinary tasks.", "Being mindful is not the same as never feeling anxious or always being happy. It means noticing what is here, including discomfort, without judging it harshly.", ""],
  ["MINDFUL ANCHORING", "K", "A practice of returning attention to a steady point, such as the breath, again and again.", "Anchoring can feel grounding, like returning to solid ground after drifting. At first the mind may wander often, which is normal and part of the practice.", "Sitting quietly with eyes soft or closed, noticing the breath at the nose or chest, and coming back each time attention drifts.", "Anchoring does not mean blanking the mind or pushing thoughts away. Each time you notice a wandering thought and return, the practice is working.", ""],
  ["MINDFUL OBSERVATION", "K", "Watching thoughts, feelings and sensations as they arise, without reacting or trying to change them.", "Observing can feel like stepping back to watch a passing scene. Strong feelings still arise, but they seem less like commands to act on right away.", "A person who names an emotion quietly, such as 'this is worry', and lets it be there rather than arguing with it or running from it.", "Observing is not the same as ignoring a problem or staying passive. It is a way to see clearly first, so that later choices are less driven by reflex.", ""],
  ["MINDFUL PRESENCE", "U", "The quality of being fully here with another person or task, rather than distracted or elsewhere.", "Presence can feel warm and settled, as if the people in front of you have your full attention. It often makes conversations feel more real and less rushed.", "Putting the phone away during a talk, making eye contact, and asking a follow-up question that shows you were listening.", "Presence is not the same as agreeing with everything someone says or staying in every hard moment. You can be fully present and still set a limit or end a talk.", ""],
  ["MINDFUL STILLNESS", "K", "A practice of sitting or standing still for a set time, noticing the body and breath without doing anything.", "Stillness can feel slow at first, with restless urges to move or check something. Over time many people find it soothing, with a sense of space opening up.", "Someone sitting quietly for a few minutes, hands resting on the lap, breathing evenly, and letting thoughts pass without following them.", "Stillness is not a test of how calm you can look. Feeling restless or sleepy during it is common, and it does not mean you are failing the practice.", ""],
  ["MINIMIZATION", "T", "A habit of shrinking the importance of your own problems, feelings or needs.", "Minimizing can bring quick relief, because the problem seems smaller. Later, the unspoken need may return as tension, guilt or a sense of being stuck.", "Phrases like 'it is not that bad' or 'others have it worse', often said before anyone has asked for a judgment about the problem.", "Putting a problem in perspective is healthy. It becomes harmful when it stops you from noticing real needs or getting help you genuinely deserve.", ""],
  ["MINIMIZING", "T", "Shrinking the size of a problem in your own mind so it feels easier to ignore.", "Minimizing can feel like a relief, because the weight seems lighter for a while. Still, the problem may keep growing quietly and surface later with more force.", "Saying 'it is no big deal' while avoiding a bill, a doctor visit or a hard conversation, or changing the subject when a concern comes up.", "Not every concern needs immediate action, and calm is not denial. It is harmful when it stops you from seeing risks that are growing or real needs that matter.", ""],
  ["MISANTHROPIC", "R", "A person who holds a deep distrust or dislike of people in general.", "Being near a misanthropic person can feel draining or cold. Others may feel judged, and conversations can seem to carry an undertone of contempt.", "Frequent comments that people are hopeless or selfish, withdrawing from social plans, and rolling eyes at ordinary kindness.", "Feeling frustrated with people at times is common and human. It becomes a problem when distrust becomes the lens for every relationship and keeps others out.", ""],
  ["MISEROPHILE", "F", "A person drawn to misery, such as sad stories, suffering or other people's pain.", "The pull toward misery can feel intense and familiar. Some people feel more comfortable with pain than with calm, so quieter moments can feel strange.", "Repeatedly seeking out tragic news, painful stories or others' suffering, and feeling restless or bored when life is steady for a while.", "Caring about suffering is not the same as enjoying it, and having a pull toward sad material is not harm in itself. It is worth noticing if it drains your energy.", ""],
  ["MOCKERY", "C", "Ridiculing or laughing at someone to make them feel small, foolish or ashamed.", "Being mocked can feel humiliating and confusing, especially when others laugh along. You may second-guess yourself, or hold back from speaking up at all.", "Jokes that target your looks, voice or choices, sarcastic 'just kidding' remarks, and mimicking you in front of others.", "Teasing that both people enjoy is different from mockery. It is harmful when one person is repeatedly made the target and told they are too sensitive.", "If this is happening to you, a counselor or domestic violence advocate can help you plan. The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["MONOMANIAC", "F", "A person who is intensely fixed on one subject and pursues it to the exclusion of most else.", "Being absorbed in one thing can feel exciting and focused. Others may feel left out, and the person may feel uneasy when the focus is interrupted.", "Talking about one topic in every conversation, neglecting work or friends, and becoming agitated when someone changes the subject.", "Deep interest is not a flaw, and many experts began as enthusiasts. It becomes a concern when the fixation costs sleep, health or important relationships.", ""],
  ["MONOPHOBIA", "P", "A strong, persistent fear of being alone, or of being left without other people nearby.", "Being alone can bring a rising panic, a sense of emptiness, or a feeling that something bad will happen. The fear can grow when no one is around.", "Constantly keeping someone nearby, avoiding time at home alone, calling people repeatedly, or leaving situations just to avoid solitude.", "Enjoying company is healthy, and needing people is human. It becomes a phobia when the fear of being alone shapes daily choices or causes distress most days.", ""],
  ["MOOD VOLATILITY", "M", "Feelings that shift quickly and strongly, often from one hour or day to the next.", "Mood can swing suddenly, leaving you feeling unsteady, raw or unsure of yourself. Small events may seem to trigger big changes that are hard to explain to others.", "Quick shifts from warm to irritable, bursts of tears or anger, and periods of energy followed by heavy tiredness, sometimes within the same day.", "Having intense feelings is not a personal failing. Frequent large swings can point to a treatable condition, and they are worth discussing with a doctor or therapist.", ""],
  ["MORAL INJURY", "M", "Deep distress that follows acting against one's values, or witnessing harm one could not prevent.", "Moral injury can bring guilt, shame, anger or a sense of being cut off from others. It may linger for months and make trust in yourself feel harder.", "Repeated self-blame over a past choice, withdrawing from people, feeling numb about things once important, or intrusive memories of what happened.", "It is not a sign that you are a bad person. It is a painful response to events that clashed with your values, and it is common among people in demanding roles.", ""],
  ["MORAL SCRUPULOSITY", "T", "Repeated worry about whether you have done something morally wrong, even when there is little reason to think so.", "Moral doubt can feel like a constant alarm, pulling you back to old choices. Relief is brief, and the next doubt often arrives within hours.", "Asking others for repeated reassurance about small ethical questions, reviewing conversations for possible wrongdoing, and apologizing again and again.", "Caring about right and wrong is good, and not everyone who worries is harmful. It becomes a problem when doubt is endless and leads to distress that does not match the facts.", ""],
  ["MORGELLONS SYNDROME", "D", "A rare, disputed condition where a person believes fibers or threads are growing under the skin.", "The belief can feel very real and frightening, with itching, crawling or stinging sensations. The person may feel dismissed when others doubt what they see.", "Persistent skin sores, collecting fibers or samples, and repeated trips to different doctors seeking proof of the problem.", "Skin symptoms are real and deserve care, even when no cause is found. Many doctors view this as a rare belief pattern, so it needs a careful medical and mental health evaluation.", ""],
  ["MOTIVATED", "U", "Having a clear drive to act and follow through on things that matter to you.", "Feeling motivated can bring energy and a sense of direction. Tasks seem more doable, and small wins feel encouraging.", "Starting a task without much prompting, planning the next step, and returning to a project after a break with interest.", "Motivation rises and falls, and it is not a requirement for every task. Waiting for it to appear before you act can keep you stuck; action often brings motivation back.", ""],
  ["MULTIPLICITY", "T", "A way of understanding the self as made of many inner parts, voices or roles that shift over time.", "Multiplicity can feel like different parts of you taking turns in charge. Some moments feel confusing, while others feel like finding a more complete picture of yourself.", "Speaking of yourself as 'a part of me wants this, another part resists', or noticing that moods and preferences change strongly by setting.", "Having many sides is part of being human, and it is not the same as a dissociative disorder. Seek a professional if you lose time, memory or a sense of who you are.", ""],
  ["MUNCHAUSEN SYNDROME", "D", "A rare condition where a person repeatedly fakes or causes illness to receive medical care and attention.", "The person may feel an urgent need for care and reassurance, often with a history of hospital stays. Underneath the illness, there is often deep distress or a longing for support.", "A long history of dramatic symptoms, many hospital visits, and details that change when doctors look more closely.", "This is not simply lying for fun, and it is not a sign of deliberate cruelty to oneself. It is a serious condition that needs a doctor and a mental health team.", ""],
  ["MUSCLE ARMORING", "B", "Chronic tightness in the muscles that some people link to holding back feelings or stress.", "Armored muscles can feel stiff, tight or heavy, as if the body is braced for something. Movement may feel limited, and rest may not fully release the tension.", "Clenched jaw, raised or hunched shoulders, tight chest or back, and a stiff, guarded posture even when relaxed.", "Body tension is real, but it is not proof of a hidden emotion. Persistent pain or stiffness should be checked by a doctor before it is attributed to feelings.", ""],
  ["MUSCLE TENSION", "B", "Muscles that stay tight or contracted, often from stress, posture or overuse.", "Tense muscles can feel sore, tight or achy, especially in the neck, shoulders or jaw. The body may feel hard to relax, even during rest.", "Raised shoulders, frowning or clenched teeth, and stiffness that worsens during stressful days or long hours at a desk.", "Ordinary tension is common and usually eases with rest and movement. It is not a sign that something is seriously wrong unless pain is severe, lasting or spreading.", ""],
  ["MUSCLE TENSION / GUARDING", "B", "Muscle tightening around an area that hurts, which the body uses to protect itself from more pain.", "Guarding can feel like the body is holding a sore area still, with tightness that you cannot easily release. Moving or pressing on the area may feel uncomfortable or alarming.", "Holding one side of the body stiffly, limping, bracing before movement, or avoiding touch in a particular area.", "Guarding is a protective response and usually reflects an injury or pain source. It is not something to push through, and sudden or severe guarding needs prompt medical care.", "If it is affecting your mood or safety, call or text 988 in the US. In an emergency, call 911."],
  ["MUSOPHILE", "F", "A person who feels a strong fondness for mice or rats, or who seeks them out often.", "The fondness can feel warm and comforting, and these animals may seem especially interesting. Others may find the interest surprising or hard to share.", "Collecting mouse or rat images, keeping several as pets, or spending free time reading and talking about them.", "Affection for small animals is harmless and common. It is only a concern if it leads to hoarding, neglect of animals, or harm to people's homes and health.", ""],
  ["MUSOPHOBIA", "P", "A strong, persistent fear of mice or rats, often out of proportion to any real danger.", "Seeing or thinking about a mouse can bring a sudden rush of panic, a racing heart, or a wish to flee. The fear can make ordinary places feel unsafe.", "Avoiding basements or kitchens, climbing onto furniture when a small animal is near, or checking constantly for signs of rodents.", "Being cautious about real rodent problems is sensible. A phobia is different, because the fear is intense and keeps the person from normal life even when there is no danger.", ""],
  ["MUTILOPHILE", "F", "A strong interest in bodily injury or amputation, sometimes with a sexual or emotional charge.", "The interest can feel intense, private and hard to explain. Many people feel shame or fear about it, which can make it harder to talk about.", "Frequently searching for images or stories about injury or amputation, and feeling a strong pull toward them that is hard to set aside.", "Having an interest is not the same as acting on it, and wanting something does not make a person dangerous. Acting on it without consent is serious harm.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis."],
  ["MUTISTIC", "D", "A pattern of being unable to speak in certain settings, even when the person can talk elsewhere.", "Being unable to speak can feel frustrating and frozen, as if the words are stuck. People may feel embarrassed or misunderstood when others expect a reply.", "Speaking freely at home but staying silent at school or work, or answering only with gestures or nods in certain places.", "This is not stubbornness or a choice to be rude. It is often linked to anxiety, and it often improves with support from a professional.", ""],
  ["MYOTONIC", "B", "A rare, inherited muscle condition where muscles are slow to relax after a contraction.", "Muscles can feel stiff or locked for a moment after use, especially after cold or sudden movement. Grip, speech or walking may feel awkward and tiring.", "Slow release after handshakes or gripping, facial stiffness, and sometimes a long-term pattern of muscle weakness or wasting.", "Stiffness is not simply being out of shape, and it is not caused by stress alone. This rare condition needs a doctor's evaluation to find the cause and plan care.", "A doctor must be seen for this condition, and a neurologist can often guide care. If it is affecting your mood or safety, call or text 988 in the US. In an emergency, call 911."],
  ["MYSOPHOBE", "P", "A person who has a strong, persistent fear of dirt, germs or contamination.", "A contamination fear can bring tension, disgust and urgency whenever something feels unclean. Relief often comes only after washing, and the fear returns quickly.", "Washing hands many times a day, avoiding shared objects or public places, and asking others to clean before touching anything.", "Caring about hygiene is healthy, and germs can cause real illness. It becomes a phobia when fear of contamination limits work, meals or relationships.", ""],
  ["MYTHOMANIAC", "F", "A person with a strong, repeated urge to tell tall tales or invent stories that are not true.", "Stories can feel exciting and bring attention or admiration. Afterward, the person may feel anxious about being caught, or ashamed of how far it went.", "Frequent dramatic claims that are hard to verify, details that keep changing, and stories that grow each time they are retold.", "Telling an occasional exaggeration is common. It becomes a concern when the stories are constant, cause harm to others or damage trust, and the person cannot stop.", ""],
  ["NARCISSISM", "R", "A pattern of strong self-focus, a need for admiration and reduced empathy for others.", "Being around narcissism can feel like your needs are always secondary. Conversations may turn back to the other person, and your own feelings can seem unimportant.", "Turning every talk toward personal achievements, expecting special treatment, and showing little curiosity about others' experiences.", "Healthy self-esteem and confidence are not narcissism. The pattern is harmful when it repeatedly ignores others, exploits them, or leaves people feeling used.", ""],
  ["NARCISSISTIC", "R", "A way of describing someone who seeks admiration, avoids criticism and often treats others as props.", "Being near a narcissistic person can feel like walking on eggshells. Compliments may come with strings, and criticism can trigger a sharp, cold response.", "Quick anger at small criticism, rewriting shared history to look better, and turning every problem into a story about their hurt.", "Not every confident or proud person is narcissistic, and a single bad day does not define someone. The label matters most when the pattern is steady and harms others.", ""],
  ["NARCISSISTIC PERSONALITY DISORDER", "D", "A personality disorder marked by a long-term pattern of inflated self-image, need for admiration and low empathy.", "People close to this condition often feel unseen, criticized or used. The person with it may feel fragile underneath, even when they appear confident.", "Lasting patterns across work and relationships, such as exaggerating achievements, reacting strongly to criticism, and taking advantage of others.", "A person with this condition is not simply arrogant or unkind. Treatment, often with therapy, can help, and a professional should make any diagnosis.", ""],
  ["NARCISSISTIC RAGE", "R", "Sudden, intense anger that follows a perceived slight, criticism or loss of admiration.", "The rage can arrive fast and feel out of proportion to the moment. Those nearby may feel scared, guilty or unsure what triggered it.", "Explosive yelling, cold silence, or harsh insults after a small disagreement, often followed by blaming others for the outburst.", "Everyone gets angry, and anger is not always abuse. It becomes harmful when rage is used to frighten or control people, or when it repeats often.", "If this is happening to you, a counselor or domestic violence advocate can help you plan. The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["NARCISSISTIC RAGER", "R", "A person who reacts to perceived slights with fierce anger that lashes out at those nearby.", "Being around a narcissistic rager can feel like walking on glass. You may rehearse every sentence in advance and still feel blamed when the anger comes.", "Sudden rage over small mistakes, insults or threats, punishing silence afterward, and blaming you for their reactions.", "A person is not defined by one angry moment, and the reasons behind anger are often complex. The pattern is harmful when it frightens or controls others.", "If this is happening to you, a counselor or domestic violence advocate can help you plan. The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["NARCOLEPSY", "D", "A long-term sleep disorder that causes sudden, uncontrollable urges to sleep during the day.", "Sleepiness can arrive without warning, sometimes mid-conversation. Many people feel exhausted even after a full night's rest, and they may feel embarrassed by it.", "Falling asleep at unexpected times, brief sleep attacks, sudden muscle weakness with strong emotion, and vivid dreams when waking.", "It is not laziness or a lack of willpower. It is a real neurological condition, and a doctor or sleep specialist can help with testing and treatment.", ""],
  ["NATURAL ENVIRONMENT PHOBIA", "P", "A strong fear of open natural places, such as forests, open fields, or the wild outdoors.", "Being in nature may bring a sense of exposure, panic or being watched. Even a park can feel threatening, and the fear may spread to nearby spaces.", "Avoiding trips outdoors, staying close to walls or buildings, or leaving a park quickly when the sky opens up or trees rise around.", "Preferring cities or indoor spaces is a normal choice. It becomes a phobia when fear of nature keeps a person from work, family life or activities they value.", ""],
  ["NATURE CONNECTION", "K", "A practice of spending time outdoors and paying attention to natural sights, sounds and rhythms.", "Time in nature can feel calming and grounding, with thoughts slowing down. Many people feel more present and less tense after even a short walk outside.", "Taking a walk without headphones, noticing trees or birds, or sitting outside to watch the sky change through the day.", "Nature connection does not require a remote wilderness or expensive trips. Small, regular time outdoors in a park or yard is enough for many people.", ""],
  ["NAUSEA", "B", "An unsettled feeling in the stomach, often with the urge to vomit, that has many possible causes.", "Nausea can feel queasy, heavy and hard to ignore. It may come with a cold sweat, reduced appetite or a sense that you might be sick.", "Pale skin, swallowing often, reduced eating, and sometimes vomiting, along with complaints of a sour or unsettled stomach.", "Nausea is a symptom, not a diagnosis, and it can come from stress, illness, medicines or pregnancy. Persistent or severe nausea should be checked by a doctor.", ""],
  ["NECROPHILIA", "F", "A rare sexual interest in dead bodies, which can cause deep distress and serious harm to others.", "The interest can feel shameful, confusing and very private. Many people feel intense fear of being discovered, and some feel isolated for years.", "Preoccupation with death-related images or thoughts that carry a sexual charge, and distress or secrecy around these urges.", "Having a disturbing interest is not the same as acting on it, and it is not a choice that defines a person. Acting on it, including with a body that cannot consent, is serious harm.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis."],
  ["NECROPHOBIA", "P", "A strong, persistent fear of dead bodies, corpses, or things that remind a person of death.", "Seeing a body, or even a picture of one, can bring panic, nausea or a wish to escape. The fear can spread to funerals, hospitals or ordinary talk about death.", "Avoiding funerals or cemeteries, turning away from images of the dead, or leaving a room quickly when death comes up.", "Feeling uneasy around the dead is common and understandable. It is a phobia when the fear keeps a person from work, family duties or health care.", ""],
  ["NEGATIVE SELF-IMAGE", "T", "A habit of seeing yourself in harsh, unfair or distorted ways, especially in appearance or worth.", "A negative self-image can feel like a constant inner critic, reminding you of flaws. Compliments may seem false, and it can be hard to feel at ease in your own skin.", "Frequent comments about your body or abilities, avoiding photos or mirrors, and dismissing praise with 'I do not really count'.", "Noticing areas to improve is healthy. It becomes a problem when the view of yourself is consistently harsh, unfair and keeps you from relationships or daily life.", ""],
  ["NEGATIVE SELF-TALK", "T", "The inner voice that repeats critical, discouraging or hopeless messages about yourself.", "Negative self-talk can feel like a steady background hum of doubt. It may leave you tired, discouraged or more ready to give up on tasks.", "Phrases like 'I always mess things up' or 'Nobody would care', repeated often, often after a small mistake.", "Everyone talks to themselves critically sometimes, and it is not a sign of weakness. It becomes harmful when the messages are constant, untrue and keep you from acting on your own needs.", ""],
  ["NEGATIVISTIC", "R", "A pattern of habitually resisting, opposing or refusing requests, even when they seem reasonable.", "Being around negativistic behavior can feel tiring and frustrating. Every plan seems to meet resistance, and simple requests can turn into long arguments.", "Saying 'no' by reflex, digging in on small points, and doing the opposite of what was asked just to avoid being directed.", "Disagreeing is healthy, and people have good reasons to push back. The pattern becomes a problem when opposition is automatic and blocks cooperation or safety.", ""],
  ["NEGGING", "C", "Subtle insults or backhanded comments that are dressed up as jokes or advice, meant to lower someone's confidence.", "Negging can leave you doubting yourself, wondering if you misheard. You may try harder to earn approval, and feel off balance around the person.", "Compliments mixed with a small put-down, such as 'you are pretty for someone who is so quiet', then a quick change of subject.", "Honest feedback and light teasing can be kind. It is harmful when the comments are repeated, hurtful and used to make someone feel they must win approval.", "If this is happening to you, a counselor or domestic violence advocate can help you plan. The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
];
